.TH RENDER 9
.SH NAME
render \- choose how a border reaches the page
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f5render\fP ?\f5direct\fP|\f5scene\fP|\f5count\fP|\f5bounds\fP|\f5nodes\fP?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Render
sets which drawing path a flat border takes, and reports which is in
force when asked with no argument.
.TP
\f5direct\fP
Straight to
.IR draw (2),
as this toolkit has always done.
This is the default.
.TP
\f5scene\fP
By building a render-node tree and rendering that.

.TP
\f5bounds\fP
Answers four numbers \(em
.BR minx ,
.BR miny ,
.BR maxx ,
.BR maxy
\(em the union of what those same trees marked, in the destination's
coordinates, and resets with the counts.
An empty rectangle means they marked nothing.
It exists because a described frame can say where it drew
.I before
anything looks at pixels, which is what lets a backend push only the part
that changed.
The union is conservative by construction, and that direction is the one
that matters: too large only costs work, too small drops pixels.
A clip node is therefore ignored rather than intersected, since a clip
can only shrink what is painted.
.TP
\f5nodes\fP
Answers two numbers: how many nodes that mark pixels went by, and how
many of those described no box.
The second should be zero.
A node that cannot say where it lands is one a backend can neither place
nor cull, which is the whole contract the tree exists to keep.
This is asked directly rather than inferred from
\f5bounds\fP because the union cannot see it: a detail node always sits
inside its own widget's background, so no scene-level rectangle changes
when a glyph run or a polygon fails to describe itself.
An ellipse is excluded from the count, its
.B r.min
being a centre rather than a corner.
.TP
\f5count\fP
Answers three numbers: how many trees the repaints since the last
\f5render count\fP rendered, how many widgets went into them, and how
many nodes those trees held.
Counting then starts again.
Batching cannot be seen in the pixels \(em that is the point of it \(em
so this is what a test reads instead.
The node count is what sizes a backend: a submission per tree is worth
making only if a tree carries enough nodes to pay for one.
The pane in
.IR tkofftest (1)
averages a couple of dozen, and prints the figure when it runs rather
than having it repeated here, where it goes stale every time a widget
is converted.
.PP
It exists to be compared against itself.
A node tree is the first piece of a renderer shaped like GTK4's GSK \(em
a retained description of a frame that something other than an immediate
.I draw
can consume \(em and the only thing that makes one worth having before a
GPU backend exists is the ability to check that it draws the same pixels.
The arrangement is the one
.IR draw3d (2)
already uses, where a software provider and a device provider are
compared against each other by
.IR sprite2dtest (1)
rather than each being trusted alone.
.PP
So this is not a setting a program has any reason to change.
It is a whole-process switch, not a property of a toplevel, because a
test wants both and an application wants neither.
.PP
.IR Tkofftest (1)
selects each in turn, reads back the offscreen pane, and requires the two
to agree byte for byte.
.SH SOURCE
.B /libtk/scene.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR style (9),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR tkbench (1)
.SH BUGS
Eleven things go through it: a flat border, a plain
.IR button (9)
with text, a
.IR radiobutton (9)
with an indicator and text, an
.IR entry (9),
selection and
.B -show
included, a
.IR listbox (9),
which needs no exception at all \(em everything it draws is a filled
rectangle, a run of text or a stroked box \(em a
.IR scale (9)
in either orientation, a
.IR scrollbar (9),
and a
.IR label (9),
several lines, an underline, a justification and a bitmap included,
which is the widget there are most of and which brings
.IR menubutton (9)
and
.IR choicebutton (9)
with it, since one draw method serves all three, a
.IR checkbutton (9)
in either state, a
.IR menu (9),
whose items are labels and whose own slab and border are nodes as well,
and a
.IR text (9),
with its clip, its glyph runs, its tag backgrounds and reliefs, its
underline and overstrike rules, its border and its caret.
Each of those draws its whole interior as nodes \(em fill, glyphs, caret,
selection band, focus ring, border.
The widget-sized ones do it inside one
.BR TkNlayer ;
a
.IR menu (9)
and a
.IR text (9)
do not, because they draw in place \(em which is why the corner mend
below matters to them and to nothing else.
.PP
Everything else \(em the classic two-tone bevel outside a
.IR text (9)'s
tags, a
.IR panel (9),
an image that came from a file descriptor rather than a path, and the
chrome that
.IR scale (9)
and
.IR scrollbar (9)
draw for themselves \(em still draws directly, so a scene is at present a
description of part of a frame rather than of a frame.
.PP
A
.IR canvas (9)
was on that list and is not.
It is the only widget whose drawing is not its own: the picture is in
the items, nine kinds of them, each with a draw method \(em so
converting the widget meant converting those.
A
.IR rectangle ,
an
.IR oval ,
a
.IR polygon ,
a
.IR bitmap ,
a
.IR line ,
a
.I text
and an
.I image
item describe themselves.
An
.I arc
and a
.I window
item draw, and each says in its own source why.
There is no node for a partial ellipse \(em
.B TkNellipse
is a whole one \(em and an arc's pieslice arm needs a temporary image
to cut its two radii against, which no node describes either.
A window item has nothing to describe at all: the picture is whatever
the embedded widget paints.
.PP
Several of the converted items describe themselves only in part, and
the part is named rather than the item excluded.
.B TkNpolyfill
fills by the nonzero winding rule and by nothing else, so a polygon
asked for another one draws; there is no node for a filled spline, so a
smooth polygon's fill draws while its outline is a
.BR TkNspline ;
a stroked polyline has no node, so only a smooth line reaches one; and
a STIPPLE draws everywhere, because it is a repeating image with the
item's centre as its source point and the ellipse and fill nodes pass
.BR ZP .
An item that draws calls
.I tkcvsdirect
first, which renders what the canvas has pending, so the order in which
items are declared is the order in which they appear \(em that is the
whole reason it takes that shape rather than being a flag on the item.
.PP
The tree is built and thrown away on every repaint, so none of the
benefit of a RETAINED tree is realised: nothing is diffed, nothing is
reused, and no animation is cheaper than it was.
What exists is the vocabulary and a renderer for it.
.PP
.B TkNborder
is a pure stroke and takes no colour for what shows outside its corners,
because a widget described entirely by nodes never needs one: its fill is
a
.B TkNroundfill
and the corner is simply never painted.
A
.I frame
works that way now.
.PP
No widget needs that any more.
A
.B TkNlayer
carries a corner radius and its composite takes that shape, so a
widget's corners are simply not copied and
.B TkNcorners
is emitted by nothing.
The type and its renderer stay, because a node type's number must not
move once a backend has seen it.
.PP
Both paths end in the same shaped blit
.RI ( tkshapedblit ,
with
.I tkinset
and
.I tkclamprad
shared so that the fill, the border and the blit cannot disagree about
where the curve is).
A widget draws itself square into a scratch and the shape is applied
when the result is copied out, so the corners of the destination are
left as they were \(em which is whatever is behind the widget.
Painting them with the parent's flat background, which is what this did
before, is right only while that is what is there: a bordered
.IR entry (9)
over a red
.IR canvas (9)
item had its corners painted page-grey over the red.
.PP
The border stroke is clamped to that shape too, in both paths.
Unclamped it draws outside the outline it has just cut away, which is
invisible until a widget is narrower than twice its own corner \(em an
8-pixel entry, a thin scrollbar \(em and then two pixels of border stick
out of a row that is otherwise not the widget at all.
.PP
The shape belongs on the composite rather than on the fill, and that
was established by trying the fill first: a rounded
.B TkNroundfill
under a square composite still showed a
.IR listbox (9)'s
selection band in the corner, because the band is drawn after the fill
and runs the full width of the widget.
Twelve pixels.
.PP
It also required the focus ring to be drawn BEFORE the border in
.IR entry (9)
and
.IR scale (9),
which is the order the other widgets already used.
A ring drawn after the border puts its square corner where the border
has just cut the widget away, so a focused entry showed a blue notch
outside its own rounded corner \(em in the ordinary drawing path, not
just in the nodes.
The border is the last word on the shape of a widget.
.PP
A
.B TkNlayer
renders the nodes that follow it into a scratch surface and blits the
result where they would have gone.
Layers do not nest, and that is not a simplification: a frame takes 27
scratch surfaces but
.I tkitmp
hands out one cached scratch per context, reused and blitted out between
widgets, so two live layers would be the same image.
One saved target is therefore sufficient.
.PP
It was proved on a
.IR frame (9),
which does not need one \(em it draws straight into its parent's image.
That was the point: the result had to be identical to rendering the same
nodes in place, so an error in the scratch's extent or the blit's offset
showed as a difference rather than as a widget that looked fine.  Every
widget that genuinely needs a layer draws with something other than
nodes, so none of them could have told us whether it worked.
.PP
A frame does not have one now, and the reason is a measurement.
A layer is a scratch the size of the widget, allocated, filled and
blitted every repaint, and a frame is usually the size of the window:
.IR tkbench (1)
put an empty toplevel \(em two frames \(em at 1.41ms on the node path
against 59us direct, and taking the layer off the frame put it back to
114us.
The six widgets that genuinely need a layer keep the node exercised.
.PP
114 against 59 was still double, and that was read for a while as the
fixed cost of the node path and left alone.
It was a frame filling the window TWICE.
An empty toplevel is one tree of exactly two nodes \(em a
.B TkNcolour
and then a
.B TkNroundfill
over the same 900\(mu700 rectangle \(em because a frame emits the corner
MEND before its fill: the rounded fill leaves the corner pixels alone,
so what is behind them has to be painted first.
At a radius of ZERO it leaves nothing alone and covers every pixel the
mend painted, and a frame with no border is the commonest widget in a
window and is usually the size of the window.
The mend is emitted only when there is a corner to mend now, and the
empty toplevel is 56.6us drawn against 56.7us through nodes \(em the gap
is gone rather than reduced, and
.IR listbox (9),
.IR button (9),
.IR entry (9)
and
.IR frame (9)
are cheaper through nodes than drawn.
A
.IR text (9)
and a
.IR canvas (9)
are 3 to 4 per cent dearer, which is what "a node per item" actually
costs.
.PP
The lesson is the one this work keeps relearning, and it cost three
measurements this time: the thing that makes a renderer slow is not the
vocabulary, it is one draw in the wrong place, and nothing but counting
finds it.
Two readings of that gap were published before this one \(em a
benchmark row that was timing a blit because its canvas was buffered,
and then a set of per-widget figures that had this very fill inside
their slope.
.PP
The one shape in the flat style that is not a rectangle is a
.IR radiobutton (9)'s
indicator, a ring with a dot in it, and the flat
.IR scale (9)'s
knob, which is two filled circles.
.B TkNellipse
and
.B TkNellipsefill
exist for those.
They use the fields differently from every other node \(em
.B r.min
is the centre and
.B sp
is the two radii \(em because an ellipse is a centre and two radii, and
a bounding rectangle cannot say whether a circle of radius
.I r
is
.I 2r
or
.I 2r+1
across.
They are numbered after the six below rather than beside their
rectangular cousins, because the numbering of a node type must not move
once a backend has seen it.
.PP
The flat
.IR scrollbar (9)'s
arrows are solid triangles, the one shape left in the flat style that is
neither a rectangle nor an ellipse, and
.B TkNpolyfill
exists for them.
Its points are copied into the scene, as a glyph node's string is, and
it always fills by the nonzero winding rule.
.PP
A
.IR checkbutton (9)'s
tick is a stroked B-spline, which is the last thing in the flat style
that no other node could describe, and
.B TkNspline
exists for it.
It owns a copy of its points as
.B TkNpolyfill
does;
.B width
is the thickness and
.B radius
carries the end style, the same one at both ends, which is what every
caller asks for.
.PP
A repaint builds one tree for each RUN of consecutive widgets that can
describe themselves as nodes, not one per widget and not one for the
whole repaint.
.I tkdrawslaves
opens a walk round its descent;
.I tkwalkjoin
hands a widget the walk's tree if one is open for that context and that
window, and
.I tkscenefinish
renders a tree only if it is not the walk's.
Anything that paints into the window itself \(em every widget that asks
.I tkitmp
for the scratch surface, and the canvas and panel painters, which draw
straight into it \(em calls
.I tkwalkflush
first, or the tree, rendered later, would land on top of what it drew.
.PP
A canvas flushes and then builds a tree of its OWN rather than joining
the walk.
Its items are usually drawn into an image the canvas keeps rather than
into the window, and a tree belongs to one target, so it renders its
tree where the items would have drawn \(em which is what
.I tkdrawrelief
already does with a one-off tree.
There are two places that happens.
Unbuffered, the items go straight into the window under a narrowed
clip; buffered, which is the DEFAULT, they go into the canvas's own
image and that is blitted afterwards.
The buffered one was covered by nothing at all until it was looked for:
a buffered canvas re-runs its item draw methods only while something in
it has changed, and a repaint that finds it clean blits what is already
there \(em so a pair of snapshots taken either side of a change of path
compared one cached image with itself, and deleting that render
outright moved no pixel of
.IR tkofftest (1).
The test dirties every canvas it can find before each snapshot now.
A
.IR menu (9)
used to be on that list and is not: its items are labels and were
already nodes, and the two things it painted itself \(em the slab behind
an item and its own border \(em have nodes too, so the walk runs through
it.
The grid clipping in
.I tkdrawslaves1
is on it and stays on it: a tree must not span a change to the
destination's own clip, because the nodes already in it were drawn
before the narrowing and would be cut by it \(em the frame's own border
lies outside the rectangle a grid clips its children to, and vanished
as soon as a gridded child flushed.
A
.IR text (9)
narrows the window's clip around its own inside, so it flushes once
before doing so \(em a tree must not span that \(em and its own drawing
is then a
.B TkNclip
with fills, glyph runs and rules inside it.
A text widget holding an embedded WINDOW needs no exception: the
descent flushes in the middle of a line, and the walk hands the clip on
to the tree that comes after.
Descents nest \(em a canvas's window item and a text widget's embedded
windows each descend from inside a draw method \(em so the walk is a
small stack.
An inner descent draws into a different window and must not join the
outer tree, and with a single state its close left the outer descent
with no walk at all: every widget after a canvas built its own tree
again.
That is a batching difference and not a drawing one, which is why the
control for it draws the same pixels and reports 11 widgets batched
instead of 15.
.PP
Painter order is therefore exactly what it was, which is what the
identity check measures;
.B "render count"
answers how many trees and how many widgets, which is what a test asks
to see the batching, since the pixels cannot show it.
.PP
.B TkNglyphs
carries the NAME of its face, not a
.BR Font* .
A font belongs to a display and can be closed under a tree that points
at it, which is why a node holding one could not outlive the call that
built it; a name is a value, and the renderer resolves it with
.I font_open
\(em the same call the toolkit uses for every face it opens, including
.BR *default* .
What it does not yet do is lay the run out into positioned glyphs, which
is the other half of that job and the half a backend needs, since a
backend cannot call
.I string
at all.
.PP
.B TkNtexture
keeps the same rule and names a FILE.
Both sources of an image in this toolkit end at a path \(em a
.B -bitmap
is
.BI /icons/tk/ name
or an
.BI @ path \fR,\fP
and
.B "image create bitmap -file"
goes through the same option parser \(em and a path is resolvable by a
renderer, which has a
.B Display
for
.I display_open
but no
.B TkTop
with which to look a Tk image name up.
The renderer opens the file and caches it, as it caches a face.
.B width
says how the image is used: 1 for a MASK, with
.B rgba
the colour painted through it, which is what a one-bit icon in the
foreground colour is; 0 for a source, drawn as it stands.
An image that came from a file DESCRIPTOR has no path and keeps the
scratch.
.PP
A clip is a property of a SPAN of nodes rather than of one, so
.B TkNclip
is bracketed the way
.B TkNlayer
is, and
.B TkNclipend
closes it.
The renderer narrows the target's clipping rectangle and puts it back,
which is what a
.IR grid (9)
does to the window by hand around its children \(em and because the
node carries the clip along with the drawing it applies to, the walk no
longer has to flush on each side of a grid to render its nodes while
the clip still held.
Clips do not nest and do not mix with a layer, whose target is a
scratch image in different coordinates.
A clip node is for a span whose tree OUTLIVES the narrowing, which is
the text widget's case and not the grid's: a grid flushes on each side
of its own narrowing, so the image's clip does the work and a node
there changes not one pixel.
The clip belongs to the WALK rather than to a tree: a tree is rendered
and freed when the walk flushes, which can happen inside the span a
clip covers, so
.I tkwalkclip
records what is open and re-emits it into whatever tree comes next.
Clips nest by intersection, innermost last, so one rectangle is all the
renderer ever sees.
A tree pointer must NOT be held across anything that paints \(em
.I tkwalkflush
frees the tree, and a
.IR menu (9)
with a separator in it faulted on a pointer built out of its own item
text before this was understood.
.PP
.B TkNclipend
is not numbered next to
.B TkNclip
because
.BR TkNclip 's
slot was reserved before its shape was settled and a node type's number
must not move once a backend has seen it.
.PP
Four node types are declared and not implemented:
.BR TkNlinear ,
.BR TkNshadow ,
.B TkNtransform
and
.BR TkNopacity .
They are written down so the numbering does not change later, and
.I tkscenerender
counts them and ignores them.
.B TkNtransform
and
.B TkNopacity
are the ones that make an animation cost nothing to rasterise, which is
the whole point of the exercise and is not done.
